<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

	<!--
		The calling orb's own incident (2026-09-22). A lit calling orb on a map at
		night already adds a dragon to the ordinary reptile wander-in (the
		<orbCandidates> line on PMM_ReptileWandersIn); this def gives the same orb a
		second, dedicated chance on top of that, so an orb standing on a desert or a
		river bank really does raise the odds there - not only on the mountains and
		caves where dragons already walk.

		No <candidates>: this def has no habitat roster at all. Its whole list is the
		orb's own <orbCandidates>, which is why the worker refuses the incident
		outright when no calling orb is lit - and why it says so in the log in
		development mode. Dragons only: no wyverns (user ruling).

		Silent spending: the worker turns the calling orb into the glowing orb on the
		roll that a dragon actually arrives on, with no message. Nothing in this
		letter mentions the orb either, so the swap is silent from the player's side
		(user ruling).
	-->
	<IncidentDef>
		<defName>PMM_DragonOrbCall</defName>
		<label>a dragon answers a dragon orb</label>
		<category>Misc</category>
		<targetTags><li>Map_PlayerHome</li></targetTags>
		<workerClass>PMM_Reptiles.IncidentWorker_ReptileWandersIn</workerClass>
		<baseChance>0.5</baseChance>
		<populationEffect>IncreaseMedium</populationEffect>
		<letterLabel>{0} answers the orb</letterLabel>
		<letterText>A wild {0} has followed the light of a dragon orb out of the dark. {PAWN_pronoun}'s called {PAWN_nameDef}.\n\nYou can attempt to tame {PAWN_objective}.</letterText>
		<letterDef>NeutralEvent</letterDef>
		<modExtensions>
			<li Class="PMM_Reptiles.ReptileWildExtension">
				<orbCandidates>
					<li>PMM_Reptile_WildDragon</li>
				</orbCandidates>
			</li>
		</modExtensions>
	</IncidentDef>

</Defs>
